  "worked_solution": "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\pi \\cdot {6}^{2}=36\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{\u222c}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{\u00a0}dA={\u222c}_{\u0394}\\text{\u00a0}f(r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta )\\,r\\text{\u00a0}drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\u0394={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\u222c}_{\u0394}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta +{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta )}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{6}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\frac{{6}^{3}}{3}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =72{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =72[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =72(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =144\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{36\\pi }\\cdot 144\\pi =4.\n$$",
